'''''Achillea maritima''''', commonly known as '''cottonweed''' or '''sea cottonweed''', is an evergreen, woolly perennial [[subshrub]] in the family [[Asteraceae]].{{sfn|RHS Gardening|2026}}{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}} The species is specialized to maritime environments, possessing [[Adaptation|physical adaptations]] to survive extreme coastal factors such as [[salt spray]], shifting [[Dune#Coastal dunes|sand dune]] substrates, and high winds.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}}{{sfn|RHS Gardening|2026}} It is widely distributed across the coastlines of the [[Mediterranean Basin]], the Atlantic shores of [[Western Europe]], the [[Canary Islands]], and parts of the [[Black Sea]] and [[Caucasus]].{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}

== Taxonomy and molecular phylogenetics ==
The species was originally described by [[Carl Linnaeus]] in his seminal 1753 work ''[[Species Plantarum]]'' under the scientific name ''Filago maritima''.{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}} Throughout the 19th and 20th centuries, due to its specialized morphological variances from other daisy family members, specifically its dense woolly covering and lack of a distinct [[Pappus (botany)|pappus]], it was frequently isolated into its own monotypic genus, most notably as ''Otanthus maritimus'' by [[Johann Centurius Hoffmannsegg]] and [[Heinrich Friedrich Link]] in 1834.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}}{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}

In 2005, the Austrian botanist Friedrich Ehrendorfer and his collaborator Yan Ping Guo published a comprehensive multi-disciplinary re-evaluation of the genus ''[[Achillea]]'' using [[molecular phylogenetics]].{{sfn|Ehrendorfer|Guo|2005|p=50}} Their DNA sequencing demonstrated that ''Otanthus'' was nested deeply within the evolutionary lineage of ''Achillea'' ''sensu lato'', making its segregation cladistically invalid.{{sfn|Ehrendorfer|Guo|2005|p=50}} Consequently, they transferred the taxon to its current accepted combination, ''Achillea maritima'' (L.) Ehrend. & Y.P.Guo.{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}

=== Infraspecific taxa ===
Two distinct subspecies are recognized:{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}

* ''Achillea maritima'' subsp. ''maritima'' – The nominate subspecies, covering the vast majority of the coastal European, Macaronesian, and Mediterranean distribution range.{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}
* ''Achillea maritima'' subsp. ''atlantica'' (Chrtek & B.Slavík) Ehrend. & Y.P.Guo – A specialized geographical variant native to the North African coastal reaches.{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}

== Description ==
''Achillea maritima'' forms a bushy, heavily branched cushion or mound that typically ranges between 10 and 50&nbsp;cm (3.9 and 19.7 in) in height.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}}{{sfn|RHS Gardening|2026}} The entire plant, including stems, leaves, and outer flower structures, is completely enveloped in a dense, velvety, snow-white to silver-grey woolly covering composed of highly interwoven tresses (a [[tomentose]] indumentum).{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}}{{sfn|RHS Gardening|2026}} This dense mat of hairs is a specialized xerophytic adaptation designed to shield plant tissues from intense sunlight, reduce water loss via [[transpiration]], and act as a physical shield against abrasive coastal sand particles.

The foliage consists of small, alternate, fleshy leaves measuring 5 to 17&nbsp;mm in length.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}} The leaves are oblong to oblong-lanceolate, sessile (lacking a stem), and feature smooth or subtly toothed margins.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}} When bruised, the foliage releases a strong, aromatic, herbal fragrance.{{sfn|RHS Gardening|2026}}

The blooming period lasts from June through September.{{sfn|RHS Gardening|2026}} The inflorescence is composed of subglobose flower heads (capitula) measuring 8 to 12&nbsp;mm wide, which are arranged in terminal clusters (corymbs) of 2 to 8.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}}{{sfn|RHS Gardening|2026}} Unlike the classic daisy profile, the capitula completely lack outer ray florets (petals). Instead, they are composed strictly of tiny, bright yellow, tubular disc florets.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}} The resulting fruits are curved, straw-colored [[achene]]s measuring 4 to 5&nbsp;mm, which completely lack a bristly pappus.{{sfn|Cretan Flora|2023}}

== Distribution, habitat, and conservation ==
The species is naturally found growing on maritime sand dunes, shingle beaches, and coastal sand bars. Its native range is highly expansive across the coastal zones of the subtropical and temperate Western Palearctic:{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}

* Atlantic and Macaronesia: Coastal [[Portugal]], Spain, France, and Ireland, alongside the islands of Gran Canaria and Lanzarote.{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}
* Mediterranean and Black Sea Basin: Ranging from the Balearic Islands, Corsica, Sardinia, and Sicily across the Balkan Peninsula, Greece, [[Turkey]], Cyprus, Syria, Lebanon, Israel, Egypt, Libya, Tunisia, Algeria, and Morocco.{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}}

Despite its wide global footprint, the species is highly sensitive to habitat degradation caused by coastal tourism, beachfront stabilization projects, and dune erosion. It is officially declared extinct in Great Britain, where historic populations on the southern English coast have completely vanished.{{sfn|RBG Kew|2026}} Highly isolated remaining populations on the northwestern limits of its range, such as those at Lady's Island Lake in County Wexford, Ireland, are targets of active conservation management to protect remaining dune systems from foot traffic and mechanical disturbance.{{sfn|Hurley|2022}}

